28.5 Controlling Interrupts

One of the earliest solutions used to provide mutual exclusion was to disable interrupts for critical sections; this solution was invented for single-processor systems. The code would look like this:

1 void lock() {

2 DisableInterrupts();

3 }

4 void unlock() {

5 EnableInterrupts();

6 }

Assume we are running on such a single-processor system. By turning off interrupts (using some kind of special hardware instruction) before entering a critical section, we ensure that the code inside the critical section will not be interrupted, and thus will execute as if it were atomic. When we are ﬁnished, we re-enable interrupts (again, via a hardware instruction) and thus the program proceeds as usual.

