TIP: LESS CODE IS BETTER CODE (LAUER’S LAW) Programmers tend to brag about how much code they wrote to do something. Doing so is fundamentally broken. What one should brag about, rather, is how little code one wrote to accomplish a given task. Short, concise code is always preferred; it is likely easier to understand and has fewer bugs. As Hugh Lauer said, when discussing the construction of the Pilot operating system: “If the same people had twice as much time, they could produce as good of a system in half the code.” [L81] We’ll call this Lauer’s Law, and it is well worth remembering. So next time you’re bragging about how much code you wrote to ﬁnish the assignment, think again, or better yet, go back, rewrite, and make the code as clear and concise as possible.

